    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Beyond the loss of appetite, have you been feeling a persistent sense of sadness or a loss of interest in the things you usually enjoy?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S024",
      "patient_response": "Yeah, I mean... sadness is one word for it, but it almost feels too small, if that makes sense. It's just... well, most mornings I wake up with this heavy, hollow feeling like the day is already ruined before I even put on my badge. \n\nAnd I mean, my alarm goes off at 5:30 AM, and usually I'd just hop right up, you know? Get the coffee going, maybe listen to the morning news, get my head in the game. But lately, I just sit on the edge of the bed staring at the floor for what feels like twenty, thirty minutes, just feeling this... this crushing weight in my chest. My ID badge is just sitting there on the dresser next to my keys, and I look at it, and I just feel completely empty about the whole thing. \n\nAnd it's not just work, either, it's everything. You asked about things I enjoy, and... well, the things I used to actually like doing on my days off are just gone for me. Like, I used to be really into fixing up old electronics, radios and things. I have this whole workbench set up in the spare room, spent a lot of time and money getting it just right over the years—but now I don't even go in there. I just don't care at all. The dust is just collecting on my tools and I can't bring myself to pick them up. It’s just this constant, draining heaviness that follows me everywhere. I try to put on a brave face at the clinic, because, you know, the patients are going through enough and they don't need to see the support staff looking miserable, but it takes every ounce of energy I have just to pretend I'm not completely hollowed out inside.",
