    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "In those two years you've been feeling this way, have you had any periods where you felt like yourself again—where the mood and the lack of interest lifted for more than two months at a time?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "No, no, I really haven't. And that's... that's the thing that really scares me, honestly. You know, you hear people talk about having a bad month or a rough patch, like maybe around the holidays or when it's dark out in the winter, and then spring comes and they bounce back. But for me, it's just been this one, unbroken, endless stretch of... of just nothingness. \n\nI keep waiting for it to lift. I kept telling myself, okay, maybe when I finally get used to the new EMR system, I'll feel better. Or maybe when they switch me back to day shifts for a week, I'll snap out of it. I'd even try to force it, you know? Like, I'd go into the spare room, look at my workbench, and say, \"Today is the day I'm going to fix that old Zenith radio.\" I'd even sit down in the chair. But then I'd just sit there for an hour, staring at the vacuum tubes, and I just couldn't make my hands move. The feeling just wouldn't go away. \n\nThere hasn't been a single month, let alone two months, where I woke up and thought, \"Hey, I feel like me again.\" It's just been this constant, low-level dread and exhaustion every single day since the schedule change. Even on my days off, even when I take PTO—which I rarely do anymore because what's the point if I'm just going to sit on the couch?—the heaviness is still right there. It never takes a vacation. It's like I'm stuck at the bottom of a well and I haven't seen the sun in two years, not even for a fleeting second. So, no, there haven't been any breaks in it at all. Not one.",
